#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289342 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2311a895-587e-5609-8a9f-9963b8cae372" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve Given in Implicit Form at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ec757bb-3dfa-50cf-a036-4b701e244974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289344} {:answer/id #uuid "182d89a3-8e68-56fd-99ea-cc5228a901c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289345} {:answer/id #uuid "47d10c09-d110-5618-87f1-9aaa11031ab0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289346} {:answer/id #uuid "2f5efcdb-4fb6-575b-8c3e-cbfad4ea791d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289347} {:answer/id #uuid "6f95090b-f1af-51e9-b801-724b96eb80e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47d10c09-d110-5618-87f1-9aaa11031ab0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289346} :answer-field/id #uuid "461fe2f6-5dcd-5d99-aa2b-10d5c980a09c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289343}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c9378bf5-2695-5a6e-bd26-2cc8bb89f62a" :question/math-academy-id "q-109152" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,1)$ for the curve $5{x}^{3}-2{y}^{4}=3.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{3}-2{y}^{4}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{y}^{4}) & =0 \\\\ 15{x}^{2}-8{y}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ -8{y}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-15{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{15{x}^{2}}{8{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,1)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(1,\\,1)} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{15{x}^{2}}{8{y}^{3}})}_{(1,\\,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\cdot {1}^{2}}{8\\cdot {1}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064395 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "387d85df-b839-5837-89b9-5e4bb4daa073" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Negative Integer Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f04a01be-80fc-5e6a-bbc9-ab4aae41e4fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(2(1 - 3x)^{2}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061055} {:answer/id #uuid "f74b4f0c-c375-556c-bc2e-d2b02b3c4e09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3)/(2(1 - 3x)^{2}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061056} {:answer/id #uuid "09e8f656-98e2-50ad-9321-cdaa37584f5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- (1)/(2(1 - 3x)^{2}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061057} {:answer/id #uuid "210d759b-1043-5395-8329-20be517c0bed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/((1 - 3x)^{2}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061058} {:answer/id #uuid "21246556-f936-52db-a0ef-85051f1e0b20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2)/(3(1 - 3x)^{2}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f04a01be-80fc-5e6a-bbc9-ab4aae41e4fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(2(1 - 3x)^{2}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061055} :answer-field/id #uuid "6da4ce87-91c1-526e-9789-f4b6790e5cd3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061054}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ff0caf62-efde-5e81-b5d3-dad00511c8fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-12019" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{3}{(1-3x)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int 3(1-3x)^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=1-3x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3(1-3x)^{-3} & =3\\int {u}^{-3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})\\int {u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\int {u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(-2)}{u}^{-2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-3x)^{-2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2(1-3x)^{2}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{2(1-3x)^{2}}+C] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{(1-3x)^{2}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(1-3x)^{-2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2)(1-3x)^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1-3x)+0 \\\\  & =-(1-3x)^{-3}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =3(1-3x)^{-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(1-3x)^{3}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289349 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2169053d-78fc-5714-82f4-d89fc36ff695" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of an Observation, Standard Deviation, or Mean Given a Z-Score"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "680f309c-f29c-5d04-b772-47562f3b779d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "680f309c-f29c-5d04-b772-47562f3b779d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289351} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8d09aa2-650f-5b7c-a37d-af9190217b56" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48d0e4ae-cc5c-5722-bf40-db66985016c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-332057" :question/problem "A data set has a mean $\\overset{―}{x}=6.2$ and an unknown standard deviation. An observation of the data set has the value $x=5.08$ and $z$-score $z=-0.7.$ Find the standard deviation of the data set.\n\n$\\sigma =$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=5.08,$ $\\overset{―}{x}=6.2,$ and $z=-0.7$ into the formula and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 5.08 & =6.2+(-0.7)\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ -1.12 & =-0.7\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =1.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289352 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "831f9aa0-099e-5e0c-8a9d-c4828b629f64" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Radians Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d32814f1-d1e0-50b7-89a9-a00a0bc611b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d32814f1-d1e0-50b7-89a9-a00a0bc611b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289354} :answer-field/id #uuid "f38c1260-42a1-543e-88d9-b37d2938d87a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289353} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05ebb977-398f-5685-b816-3d967ddaddb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05ebb977-398f-5685-b816-3d967ddaddb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289356} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cb72f22-101f-5fc1-9ed5-76b535fd3701" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2045d655-d8e8-53aa-8739-aa078fd23538" :question/math-academy-id "q-332518" :question/problem "Express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{7\\pi }{4})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{7\\pi }{4})=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289357 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e37b91f0-ea0a-58b0-98d0-8f3626578dd6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit of Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bfc4274-fe83-5def-b030-97c4e642e606" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bfc4274-fe83-5def-b030-97c4e642e606" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289359} :answer-field/id #uuid "02590595-12a1-572b-b24b-b038aa655c21" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1f95a9f2-42f7-5f98-8ff5-f6c60ffeb68d" :question/math-academy-id "q-344110" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/assets/1b564f91cb71a17a1449e1ff723e32f450da6304d9c3ba33cd01e2ff942bc25c.png)\n\nAs $x$ approaches $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ from the right, the values of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ are positive and grow without bound. Therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060270 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b05f4be1-95d4-5137-bbaa-71c95862bb59" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions by Factoring an Alternate Form of the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a778ca1a-128d-5874-9ee2-3ac3ce2ce9e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058604} {:answer/id #uuid "b7ba89df-7791-5d31-a1b0-3c8a1cc4f575" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 - \\sin x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058605} {:answer/id #uuid "fdf43fe7-371a-5c9f-8a42-d5a6573926fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sin^{2} x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058606} {:answer/id #uuid "3155df93-b04e-56b6-82ed-d400cb59a00e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 + \\sin x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058607} {:answer/id #uuid "7d9a53df-6b30-5f0a-a454-58d89d985d2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sin x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7ba89df-7791-5d31-a1b0-3c8a1cc4f575" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 - \\sin x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058605} :answer-field/id #uuid "310a746d-cdba-54e9-97c1-9571d933160e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c024ed7c-9654-5da5-b602-2f368dd77de2" :question/math-academy-id "q-65181" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the numerator using $1-{sin}^{2}⁡x={cos}^{2}⁡x.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}=\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the numerator as a difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1} & =\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1} \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083784 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8dbb426-dd34-5686-bde9-3ef9d246b5e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad9ff572-8ddf-5430-b5d1-f44c4baed2f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[MATH: f(x) ∈ (- ∞,-2]∪[0, ∞)]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082071} {:answer/id #uuid "cdfd952f-1003-5186-a06a-db73e00275de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[MATH: f(x) ∈ (- ∞, 0]∪[1, ∞)]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082072} {:answer/id #uuid "d9b000a3-a0a7-5bc1-8574-a763e6ae468b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[MATH: f(x) ∈ (- ∞, 0]∪[2, ∞)]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082073} {:answer/id #uuid "5793a38f-845f-57d5-8d29-3e1d62fcd848" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[MATH: f(x) ∈ (- ∞,-2]∪[2, ∞)]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082074} {:answer/id #uuid "717ac43c-1575-5b85-9446-317a4571683f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[MATH: f(x) ∈ (- ∞,-1]∪[2, ∞)]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082075}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9b000a3-a0a7-5bc1-8574-a763e6ae468b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[MATH: f(x) ∈ (- ∞, 0]∪[2, ∞)]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082073} :answer-field/id #uuid "93a6f974-053c-5c88-86f3-155ac1eec22b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082070}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6866850c-c810-557a-85a3-faf1a7a87a61" :question/math-academy-id "q-65951" :question/problem "Find the range of the function $f(x)=-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+1.$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- Multiplying the first interval by $-1$ and adding $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1 \\\\  & -\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\ge 1 \\\\  & -\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+1\\ge 1+1 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second interval by $-1$ and adding $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\ge 1 \\\\  & -\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1 \\\\  & -\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+1\\le -1+1 \\\\  & f(x)\\le 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get \n\n$$\nf(x)\\ge 2,\\,f(x)\\le 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,0]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$"}]}
